---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CoinFlip from '@/components/tools/everyday/CoinFlip.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="coin-flip">
  <CoinFlip client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use the coin flipper</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Press <strong>Flip once</strong> for a single animated toss.</li>
      <li>
        Set a batch size and press the batch button to run up to 10,000 flips at once. The page shows
        progress and stays responsive, because the flips are processed in chunks.
      </li>
      <li>
        Watch the fairness chart: it plots the running share of heads from the first flip to the last,
        against the 50% line.
      </li>
      <li>Press <strong>Reset</strong> to clear the tally and start a new experiment.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Is this coin actually fair?</h2>
    <p>
      Yes. Each flip reads a new 32-bit value from <code>crypto.getRandomValues</code>, the
      cryptographically secure random number generator of the browser, seeded by the operating system.
      Rejection sampling maps the value to heads or tails, so the mapping adds no bias. There is no
      predictable seed, no dependence on the clock, and no memory of previous flips.
    </p>
    <p>
      The coin does not remember its last result. After nine heads, the tenth flip is still exactly
      50/50.
    </p>

    <h2>Streaks are normal; the gambler's fallacy is not</h2>
    <p>
      The belief that a run of heads makes tails "due" is the gambler's fallacy, the most common
      misreading of randomness. Long runs are expected:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Number of flips</th>
            <th scope="col">Expected longest run</th>
            <th scope="col">Chance of a run of 5+</th>
            <th scope="col">Chance of a run of 10+</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>10</td><td>~3</td><td>~22%</td><td>~0.2%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>100</td><td>~6–7</td><td>~97%</td><td>~5%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1,000</td><td>~9–10</td><td>&gt;99.9%</td><td>~38%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>10,000</td><td>~13</td><td>&gt;99.9%</td><td>~99%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Run a batch of ten thousand here and compare the longest-run figure with the table. A sequence
      with no long runs is suspicious: it is typical of a person who tries to fake randomness.
    </p>

    <h2>How the proportion converges</h2>
    <p>
      The law of large numbers says that the proportion of heads approaches 50%. It does not say that
      the counts get closer. The absolute gap usually grows, roughly with the square root of the number
      of flips:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Flips</th>
            <th scope="col">Typical gap from an even split</th>
            <th scope="col">Typical gap as a percentage</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>100</td><td>±5 flips</td><td>±5.0%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1,000</td><td>±16 flips</td><td>±1.6%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>10,000</td><td>±50 flips</td><td>±0.5%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>100,000</td><td>±158 flips</td><td>±0.16%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      So 5,050 heads out of 10,000 is a 50-flip gap, which is ordinary, and as 50.5% it looks very close
      to fair. Both describe the same result. The fairness chart on this page shows this.
    </p>

    <h2>Real coins are slightly unfair</h2>
    <p>
      A physical coin toss is close to fair but not perfectly fair. Careful experiments, including a
      study of hundreds of thousands of tosses, found a small bias of about 51% towards the side that
      faced up at the start. The cause is precession: the coin wobbles about an axis and does not spin
      cleanly, so the starting face is up for slightly more time. A coin spun on a table has much more
      bias, because the rim is not symmetrical.
    </p>
    <p>A digital flip has no such physical structure.</p>

    <h2>Good uses for a coin flip</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Settling a two-way choice quickly and visibly fairly</li>
      <li>Deciding who goes first in a game</li>
      <li>Teaching probability: the batch mode and the chart do the demonstration</li>
      <li>
        Breaking a real tie in a decision: notice how you feel about the result, which often shows what
        you wanted from the start
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      Flips are generated and counted in your browser. Nothing is sent to a server, and the tally is
      deleted when you close the tab.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
